    <p>adaptive channels as in the original adaptive routing.</p>
    <p>Once again the virtual channel allocation is crucial for deadlock avoidance. In the fault-tolerant adaptive routing, channels <span data-field="vc_a">{{ vc_a }}</span> to <span data-field="vc_b">{{ vc_b }}</span> are used for messages around f-rings. Virtual channels on links that are not on f-rings are used for normal routing by Adaptive-Algorithm. The four virtual channels on physical channels not on f-rings are partitioned into nonadaptive and adaptive subsets. The channels in the nonadaptive category are <span data-field="vc_non_1">{{ vc_non_1 }}</span>, which are used to ensure deadlock free routing. Furthermore, only one type of messages may use the nonadaptive channel on a physical channel not on an f-ring. Thus, no virtual channel can be used for normal fault-free routing by one message and for misrouting by another. This is the same as in the non-adaptive case. The adaptive channels are used for misrouting only. This is similar to that of the nonadaptive case. Figure 7 gives an example of our method. The message uses speci c channels for its hops on the f-ring links (1,1)-(1,2), (3,4)-(3,5), and (3,5)-(4,5). For its other hops, the message uses virtual channels as per the original adaptive algorithm.</p>
